    .sidebar ol {border-left: none}

.formatpagenumber {i}

.center
    Department of Applied Mathematics, ETH Zürich

.abstract
    We study energy transfer in weakly coupled anharmonic lattices near resonance.
    By applying a multiscale asymptotic expansion to the Hamiltonian, we derive a sharp
    upper bound on the lifetime of metastable resonant states. Our result bridges the
    Nekhoroshev stability regime and the onset of Chirikov diffusion, providing the first
    explicit decay rate for systems with three or more degrees of freedom.

## Introduction

Consider a Hamiltonian system of $ N $ coupled oscillators with frequencies
$ \omega_1, \dots, \omega_N $ satisfying a Diophantine condition of order $ \tau $.
When a small nonlinear perturbation $ \varepsilon H_1 $ is introduced,
the classical KAM theorem guarantees the persistence of most invariant tori.

However, near low-order resonances where $ \langle k, \omega \rangle \approx 0 $
for some $ k \in \mathbb{Z}^N $, energy can migrate between modes
on timescales far shorter than the Nekhoroshev estimate.

## Main result

Let $ E_j(t) $ denote the energy of the $ j $-th mode. We show that the resonant
energy exchange satisfies .ref {eq:energy-exchange} up to timescales of order $ T^{*} $ given by .ref {eq:lifetime}:

$ E_j(t) = E_j(0) + \varepsilon^{1/2} \sum_{|k| \leq K} A_k \, e^{i \langle k, \omega \rangle t} \cdot \frac{\sin(\pi \langle k, \omega \rangle \, t)}{\pi \langle k, \omega \rangle} + \mathcal{O}(\varepsilon) $ {#eq:energy-exchange}

where the amplitudes $ A_k $ depend on the initial actions through
$ A_k = \partial_{I_j} \hat{H}_1(k, I) $ and the truncation $ K $
is chosen to optimize the remainder.
This yields a metastable lifetime scaling as

$ T^{*} \sim \exp\!\Big(\frac{c}{\varepsilon^{1/(2\tau + 2)}}\Big) $ {#eq:lifetime}

which interpolates between the Nekhoroshev exponent $ a = \frac{1}{2N} $
and numerical observations of Chirikov diffusion onset.

## Numerical verification

We integrate the equations of motion for $ N = 3 $ oscillators
with frequencies $ \omega~=~(1, \sqrt{2}, \sqrt{5}) $ using a
symplectic Störmer--Verlet scheme at step size $ h~=~0.01 $.

| $ \varepsilon $ | $ T^{*} $ (predicted) | $ T^{*} $ (observed) |
|---|---|---|
| $ 10^{-2} $ | $ 1.4 \times 10^{3} $ | $ 1.6 \times 10^{3} $ |
| $ 10^{-3} $ | $ 2.7 \times 10^{5} $ | $ 3.1 \times 10^{5} $ |
| $ 10^{-4} $ | $ 8.9 \times 10^{8} $ | $ 7.2 \times 10^{8} $ |
"Metastable lifetimes for varying perturbation strengths."

The agreement confirms the predicted scaling within a factor of two
across three orders of magnitude in $ \varepsilon $.
